\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Perfection of the exterior pairing}
\date{2026-11-17}

\p{The \vocabk{internal exterior module}{fdip-00YE} is finite
projective, and its exterior pairing is perfect and symmetric.}

\proof{
\p{Choose a module #{V'} with #{F=V\oplus V'\cong k^N}.
Expanding an exterior product into its #{V} and #{V'} factors and
moving the #{V} factors first gives
##{\Lambda^pF\cong\bigoplus_{i+j=p}\Lambda^iV\otimes\Lambda^jV'.}
Multiplication of the two exterior factors is the inverse: expansion
and reordering on pure wedges verify both composites. In particular
#{\Lambda^pV} is a direct summand of the finite free module
#{\Lambda^pF}. It is finite projective and vanishes for #{p>N}.
Their finite direct sum #{E} is therefore finite projective.}

\p{The canonical determinant evaluation between
#{\Lambda^pF^*} and #{\Lambda^pF} is perfect: exterior products of
dual basis vectors pair with exterior basis vectors by the identity
matrix. Under #{F^*=V^*\oplus(V')^*} and the displayed decompositions,
summands with different numbers of #{V} factors are orthogonal.
Indeed a determinant term must pair every #{V} factor with a
#{V^*} factor and every #{V'} factor with a #{(V')^*} factor. The
isomorphism to the dual is consequently block diagonal. Each block
is an isomorphism, including the block
#{\Lambda^p(V^*)\to(\Lambda^pV)^*}.}

\p{The isomorphism #{b^\flat:V\to V^*},
#{b^\flat(v)=b(v,-)}, induces the isomorphism
#{\Lambda^p(b^\flat)}. Composing it with that canonical block is
exactly the map to the dual defined by the determinant pairing.
Thus the pairing is perfect in every degree. Transposing its matrix
of entries #{b(v_i,w_j)} proves symmetry. Orthogonal finite direct
sum preserves perfection and symmetry.}
}
